Supreme People's Court

Specific Provisions on Arrest of Ships Before Litigation

(Adopted by the Adjudication Committee of the Supreme People's Court on January 31, 1986)

A maritime claimant may, before instituting a maritime action, apply to a court for the arrest of the relevant ship to preserve the exercise of his maritime claim. In order to protect promptly the lawful rights and interests of parties to maritime disputes and to promote the development of international economic trade and international shipping, these specific provisions for the implementation of arrest of ships before litigation by maritime courts of China are hereby formulated in accordance with the basic principles of the Civil Procedure Law of the PRC (for Trial Implementation), on the basis of summing up trial experience, and with reference to international practice:

1. Scope of Maritime Claims

Maritime claims means claims for compensation arising from or relating to the construction, sale, purchase, chartering, operation, navigation, or salvage of seagoing ships, as well as claims relating to ownership, possession, mortgage, or maritime lien on a ship, for example:

(1) Claims arising from damage caused by collision of ships or other accidents;

(2) Claims arising from personal injury caused by a ship or by the operation or navigation of a ship;

(3) Claims arising from damage caused by pollution of the sea area by a ship;

(4) Claims arising from measures taken to prevent imminent pollution or to mitigate or eliminate pollution damage;

(5) Claims arising from salvage operations, or from the raising, removal, or destruction of a wreck or its cargo or flotsam;

(6) Claims arising from a charter party;

(7) Claims arising from a contract of carriage of goods or passengers, or from loss of or damage to goods or luggage carried by a ship;

(8) Claims arising from general average;

(9) Claims arising from towage or pilotage;

(10) Claims arising from the supply of foodstuffs, articles, materials, fuel, equipment (including containers), or services needed for the operation or routine maintenance of a ship;

(11) Claims for port charges, canal dues, or channel fees;

(12) Claims arising from the construction, repair, conversion, or outfitting of a ship;

(13) Claims arising from a mortgage on a ship;

(14) Claims arising from a marine insurance contract;

(15) Claims for wages and social insurance premiums and other sums due to the master and crew;

(16) Claims arising from payments made by the master, shipowner, bareboat charterer, other charterer, or agent on behalf of the ship;

(17) Claims arising from commissions, brokerage, or agency fees paid by the shipowner, bareboat charterer, or others on their behalf for the ship;

(18) Claims arising from the ownership or possession of a ship;

(19) Claims arising from the operation or distribution of earnings of a ship among co-owners;

(20) Claims arising from a contract for the sale of a ship.

2. Scope of Ships Liable to Arrest

A party having a maritime claim as listed in the preceding article may apply to a maritime court of China for the arrest of the particular ship in respect of which the maritime claim arose, subject to two conditions: first, that the owner of that particular ship is liable for the maritime claim; and second, that, except in the case of an application for arrest for the purpose of exercising a maritime lien, the particular ship at the time of the application for arrest and at the time the maritime claim arose is owned by the same person. He may also apply for the arrest of any other ship currently owned by the person who is liable for the maritime claim, being the shipowner, operator, or charterer; provided, however, that if the maritime claim arises from the ownership, mortgage, or operation or distribution of earnings of a ship, only the particular ship in respect of which the claim arose may be arrested.

Warships or government ships used exclusively for public service shall not be arrested.

3. Procedure for Applying for Arrest of a Ship

An application for arrest of a ship shall not be subject to any agreement between the parties concerning jurisdiction, arbitration, or governing law in respect of that maritime claim.

A maritime claimant applying for arrest of a ship shall submit a written application to a maritime court and provide evidence of the maritime claim, such as documents of title or debt, contracts, bills of lading, tally reports, loading and discharge records, marine reports, damage surveys of the ship, cargo survey reports, reports on sea area pollution, notices of loss or damage to cargo, photographs, etc.

After receiving the application for arrest of a ship, the maritime court shall examine it. If it finds that the application meets the conditions for arrest of a ship and is supported by reasonable grounds, it shall, with the approval of the president, promptly render a ruling approving the arrest. In urgent circumstances, a ruling approving the arrest shall be made within forty-eight hours, and an order for arrest of the ship shall be issued simultaneously. In the ruling approving the arrest, the person against whom the application is made shall be ordered to provide security.

After the person against whom the application is made provides reliable and sufficient security, if the maritime court approves it upon examination, it shall, with the approval of the president, promptly issue an order for release of the arrest.

The provision of security by the person against whom the application is made to obtain the release of the arrested ship shall not be deemed an admission of liability or a waiver of the right to limit liability.

Before rendering a ruling approving the arrest, the maritime court may order the applicant to provide security to compensate the person against whom the application is made for any loss caused by an erroneous application. If the applicant refuses to provide security, the application shall be rejected.

The type, form, and amount of the security shall be determined by the maritime court.

4. Service and Execution

Legal documents for arresting a ship and releasing a ship from arrest shall be served by enforcement officers of the maritime court on the master of the ship under arrest, who shall sign the service receipt. When boarding the ship to serve the order for arrest of the ship or the order for release of arrest, the enforcement officer shall read the order aloud to the master and shall post the order on the lower part of the mainmast or in another conspicuous location of the ship. When performing service duties, enforcement officers shall wear uniforms and present their "Enforcement Service Certificate."

At the same time as issuing the order for arrest of a ship or the order for release of arrest, the maritime court shall issue a notice of assistance in enforcement to the harbor superintendency administration, border control, and other maritime patrol and surveillance authorities. When necessary, the maritime court may dispatch judicial police or arrange for custodians to board the ship to execute the arrest.

5. Fees for Arrest of a Ship

The application fee for arrest of a ship shall be RMB 1,000, to be paid by the applicant. Other expenses incurred in executing the arrest shall be borne by the person against whom the application is made; if the application is erroneous, such expenses shall be borne by the applicant.

6. Arrest of a Ship and Litigation

The maritime court that arrested the ship shall have jurisdiction over the action brought on the basis of the maritime claim.

After the person against whom the application is made provides security and the arrested ship is released, the parties may also bring an action in another court having jurisdiction in accordance with an original agreement on jurisdiction, or submit the dispute to arbitration in accordance with an original arbitration agreement.

The period of arrest for preserving a maritime claim shall be thirty days. If the applicant brings an action in the maritime court that arrested the ship within this period, the proceedings shall enter the ordinary procedure.

If, within the period of arrest, the person against whom the application is made fails to provide security and the applicant fails to bring an action, the maritime court that arrested the ship shall release the arrested ship. Any loss suffered by the person against whom the application is made due to the arrest of the ship shall be borne by the applicant. If the person against whom the application is made has provided security but the applicant fails to bring an action within the period of arrest, the person against whom the application is made may apply for the return of the security provided.
